  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Friday...\n\nNear to above normal temperatures under mostly sunny skies exist \ntoday. Over south-eastern Carter County this afternoon, there is a\nslight chance (15 percent) of showers. Otherwise, dry conditions \nare expected to prevail. As the mostly clear skies continue into \ntonight, there is a slight chance (20 percent) of patchy fog over \nportions of east-central Montana. Any fog that does develop would \ndissipate shortly after sunrise on Sunday.\n\nSunday through Sunday night, the chance of precipitation returns \nto the forecast across much of the area as the low pressure system\ncurrently sitting over the Pacific Northwest approaches our area.\nIn the morning, there is a slight chance of showers (15 percent) \nover Carter and Fallon Counties in eastern Montana. As \ntemperatures warm into the 80s and 90s by mid-day, warm air aloft \nwill initially inhibit any showers and thunderstorms for most \nduring the afternoon and early evening. With that said, \ntemperatures may become warm enough to break the cap over far \neastern Montana into North Dakota and South Dakota during the late\nafternoon and early evening. With this, there is a Marginal Risk \n(Level 1 out of 5) for a few strong to severe thunderstorms. The \nmain threats are strong wind gusts, heavy rain, and hail. Sunday \nevening into Sunday night, a shortwave trough rounding the low \npressure system to the west is then expected to move through the \narea, bringing a round of scattered showers and thunderstorms to \nat least south- central Montana (up to 70 percent chance of \nprecipitation). Late Sunday night into early Monday morning, \nlingering showers and weak thunderstorms may continue east into \neastern Montana (around a 20 percent chance). With the activity \nSunday night, gusty winds and heavy rain are the main threats.\n\nMonday, the aforementioned low pressure system will move \nnortheastward through Montana. This will bring another round of \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms to much of the \narea during the day. With this, the chance of a strong to severe \nthunderstorm is very low. The main hazard looks to be the synoptic\nwinds associated with the low pressure system. At this time, \nwidespread wind gusts in the 20s to 40s mph are forecast, \nstrongest over the foothills west of Billings. Areas around\nLivingston to Big Timber and Harlowton may even see a few 50 mph\nwind gusts late Monday (50 to 60 percent chance). \n\nTuesday, windy conditions look to continue across much of the \narea, though the strongest winds will shift to be over far eastern\nMontana. Again, wind gusts in the 20s to 40s mph are forecast to \nbe common, strongest to the north and east, with a few 50 mph wind\ngusts possible around Baker, Montana (50 percent chance).\n\nWednesday into Friday are forecast to be mostly dry days as low \namplitude ridging nudges into the region aloft. By late Friday \ninto the weekend, the chance of precipitation returns, but \nuncertainty remains regarding the upper level weather pattern. LA",
